Exercise 1.27 defines \(X\subseteq|\mathcal{D}|\) to be bounded when some element lies above every member, sets \(\bigsqcup X\) equal to the intersection of all upper bounds, and asks to prove that this intersection is the least upper bound of a bounded set, that neighbourhoods \(U\) and \(V\) are consistent if and only if \(\{\uparrow U,\uparrow V\}\) is bounded, and, by Exercise 1.18, that \(X\) is bounded if and only if every finite subset is bounded. `Bounded` and `upperBounds` are those definitions. `sSup` is the least upper bound, realized as `sInf` of the upper bounds, and the `sInf` of Exercise 1.18 is the filter of neighbourhoods common to every member of a nonempty family, which is the intersection Scott writes; `upperBounds_nonempty` turns a bound into that nonempty hypothesis. `le_sSup` places each member under the least upper bound by the lower-bound law of the infimum, and `sSup_le` places the least upper bound under any upper bound by the upper-bound law. `pairSeq` is the two-term sequence with value \(U\) at index zero and \(W\) afterward, `interUpTo_pairSeq` identifies the running intersection of the first two terms with the master intersected with \(U\) and with \(W\), and `consistent_pair_iff_bounded` is the consistency comparison: a neighbourhood under that running intersection has principal filter above both principal filters, while a bound of the two principal filters contains both neighbourhoods and therefore their intersection, which witnesses consistency of the sequence. `bounded_iff_finite_bounded` reuses a global bound on every subset in one direction; in the other it collects the neighbourhoods that belong to some member of \(X\), obtains finite consistency because any finite sequence from that family lies in finitely many members and hence under one bound given by hypothesis, and takes `leastFilter` from Exercise 1.18 as an upper bound of \(X\). **Theorem:** yes — boundedness, the least-upper-bound formula, the consistency comparison, and the reduction to finite subsets are all stated. **Proof:** yes — the infimum laws, both directions of the pair criterion, and the least-filter construction are the arguments the exercise requests.
